From: Maago <maago@example.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:37:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Sign in
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

main uses the auth app. Now people sign up, verify with the link in the
mail, and sign in.

- auth.App goes into Config.Apps, and auth.Current into Config.Auth.
  For each request it finds the user of the session cookie (or of the
  API token)
- A view says in its signature whether it wants the user. Home shows
  who is signed in:

      func Home(ctx context.Context, u *auth.User, db maago.DB, in struct{}) (HomePage, error)

  The order is fixed: ctx, then the user, then db (or tx), then in,
  always last; each of the middle two only when the view needs it. The
  user's type is the one Config.Auth returns, here auth.User, and every
  view takes the same type, or maago gen says so. A pointer, *auth.User,
  is for a page anybody may see: it is nil when nobody is signed in
- LoginURL is "/app/login": a page that needs a user sends the browser
  there. Write it with the prefix, as is: maago does not add it
- auth.SiteURL begins the links in the mails. It is not built from the
  request's Host, which anybody can send. Here it is BOARD_SITE_URL, or
  http://localhost:8000 for development. Its scheme also decides the
  session cookie's Secure: http:// leaves it off, so the cookie works on
  localhost; https:// in production sets it
- authlib.ConsoleMail writes the mails to the log. A real server uses
  authlib.SMTP(addr, from, auth)
- The first staff account comes from main's -createsuperuser:

      MAAGO_SUPERUSER_EMAIL=me@example.com MAAGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=… go run . -createsuperuser

  It asks nothing, and takes no password in a flag (ps and the shell's
  history would keep it). They are variables of the shell, not of .env,
  and it runs after maago migrate
- The header links to sign in, sign up and sign out with {{url}} and
  auth's route names

Know one thing: the limit on attempts sees the IP only as r.RemoteAddr.
Behind a proxy, every request comes from the proxy. Then wrap the
Handler in main in a middleware that sets r.RemoteAddr from the proxy's
header (X-Forwarded-For or the like) for requests from the proxy's own
address alone: anybody can send that header.

The test hashes fast (authlib.Iterations), adds a verified user, POSTs
to /login, and sees the home page with the cookie it got. auth/'s own
tests already walk sign-up and verification.

